<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Padri War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Minangkabau War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Padri War was a conflict in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> from 1803 to 1837. It began as a struggle between the Padri, Islamic reformers influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahhabism">Wahhabism</a>, and the Minangkabau nobility who upheld customary law; from 1821 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> intervened on the side of the nobles. Led by Tuanku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Bonjol, the Padri held out until the fall of Bonjol in 1837, after which the highlands came under Dutch rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/padri-war.jpg" alt="Padri War" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.P. de Veer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naar-beide-zijden-front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Java War it fought the <b>Padri War</b> in West Sumatra until 1837 and then turned to the outer islands, where most of the archipelago still lay beyond Dutch control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Padri War</b>, reformist Muslims killed much of the royal family around 1815.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagaruyung-kingdom">Pagaruyung Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
